'''''Star Wars: Complete Locations''''' is a [[canon]] reference book and a reissue of the [[2005]] [[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2005)|edition of the same name]], also including new content from [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']]. The title was published by [[Dorling Kindersley]] on [[September 27]], [[2016]].

==Publisher's summary==
'' '''''Star Wars(TM): Complete Locations'' brings together the key buildings, environments, and battle scenes from the ''Star Wars'' saga in a single, definitive guide. This updated edition features four new cross-section spreads and a behind-the scenes look at how the artwork is crafted.'''''

''Packed with more than 50 full-color, intricate cross-section artwork and 3-D maps, ''Star Wars(TM): Complete Locations'' is an amazing visual guide into the incredible worlds and locations of a galaxy far, far away&hellip;.''

''From the towering skyscrapers of Coruscant to the terrifying technology of the Death Star, see in astonishing detail the numerous worlds, bases, cities, landscapes, and battles from the ''Star Wars'' galaxy. Discover the secrets of the Jedi Temple Complex and tour Yoda's House on Dagobah''.

''With brand-new cross-section artwork and sneak peek behind-the-scenes pages showing how the amazing artworks are crafted, ''Star Wars: Complete Locations'' is the definitive one-volume collection that takes you inside the world of the ''Star Wars'' saga.''

==Continuity==
*The name of the [[Abyssin]] [[species]] is misspelled "Abbysin" in this book, although author [[Jason Fry]] has explained it's merely a typo.
*[[Coruscant]] is said to have 5217 levels, though it actually has [[Level 5127|5127]].<ref name="TCaJ EG">{{SW|url=series/clone-wars/to-catch-a-jedi-episode-guide|text="To Catch a Jedi" Episode Guide &#124; The Clone Wars}}</ref> This was corrected in later releases.
*''Complete Locations'' states that the [[Delta-7B Aethersprite-class light interceptor|Delta-7 ''Aethersprite''-class light interceptor]] was created in response to the [[Invasion of Naboo]]. However, the [[Star Wars comic books|comic book]] [[Star Wars (2015) 26|''Star Wars'' (2015) 26]], set before the Invasion of [[Naboo]], featured Delta 7s.<ref name="Star Wars 26">[[Star Wars (2015) 26|''Star Wars'' (2015) 26]]</ref>
*[[Endor]]'s population is said to be "Unknown." However, ''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]'' previously stated it to be over 30,000,000 [[Ewok]]s.<ref name="SW:AEYNtK">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]''</ref>
*[[Geonosis]]' population is stated to be 1 billion whereas ''Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know'' states it to be 100 billion.<ref name="SW:AEYNtK" /> ''Complete Locations'' also states there was no population on Geonosis during the [[Imperial Era]]. This contradicts the ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]'' episode "[[Ghosts of Geonosis]]," where [[Klik-Klak]], the sole survivor of the [[Galactic Empire]]'s [[sterilization of Geonosis]], is protecting a [[Karina|Queen egg]].<ref name="Ghosts of Geonosis">{{Rebels|Ghosts of Geonosis}}</ref>
*The planet profile for [[Kashyyyk]] mistakenly uses an image of [[Dagobah]].
*[[Lott Dod]] is stated to have been present during the [[First Battle of Geonosis]] in the archducal box of the [[Petranaki Arena]]. Though [[Star Wars: Episode II Attack of the Clones|Episode II]] identified that [[Neimoidian]] as Dod, the Neimoidian is assumed to be [[Rune Haako]].<ref name="Episode II">{{Film|II}}</ref>
*While ''Complete Locations'' states that Naboo only has one [[moon]], [[Mace Windu]] mentions "spice miners on the moons of Naboo" in ''Star Wars'': Episode II ''Attack of The Clones''.<ref name="Episode II" /> ''[[Thrawn: Alliances]]'' later stated that Naboo has three moons.<ref name="Thrawn: Alliances">''[[Thrawn: Alliances]]''</ref>
*[[Monarch of Naboo|King]] [[Ars Veruna]] is said to have reigned for thirteen years. However, ''[[Queen's Shadow]]'' later established that Naboo's monarch could reign for a maximum of four years.<ref name="Q'sS">''[[Queen's Shadow]]''</ref>
*[[Yoda's Hut]] is stated to have been destroyed by Dagobah's torrential rains after [[Yoda]]'s [[death]]. However, the [[2015]] reference book ''[[Ultimate Star Wars]]'' previously stated that it was reclaimed by the swamp and the many creatures Yoda befriended during his [[Exile (punishment)|exile]].<ref name="Ultimate SW">''[[Ultimate Star Wars]]''</ref> After being asked about this, [[Leland Chee]] responded that the information of both sources can be canonical, explaining that the hut could have been reclaimed right after Yoda's death but destroyed a hundred years later.<ref name="Chee's Tweet">{{Twitter|HolocronKeeper|status/1095020412138704896|[[Leland Chee]]|quote=I was thinking in-universe date. Someone writing the passage right after RotJ would say that it was reclaimed. 100 years later it might be destroyed.|archivedate=20190722103037}}</ref>
*[[Yoda's lightsaber]] is revealed to have been stored in a box at Yoda's Hut in [[3 ABY]]. However, the comic [[Darth Vader (2017) 1|''Darth Vader'' (2017) 1]] states that the [[lightsaber]] was destroyed during the [[celebration marking the eradication of the Jedi Order]] in [[19 BBY]].<ref name="DV: Dark Lord 1">[[Darth Vader (2017) 1|''Darth Vader'' (2017) 1]]</ref> When asked about this, writer [[Charles Soule]] mused that perhaps Yoda built a new one, that he had more than one or that Darth Sidious built a fake to make a point,<ref name="Soule's Tweet">{{Twitter|CharlesSoule|status/1094940389306499072|[[Charles Soule]]|quote=He built a new one. Or he had more than one. Or Palpatine made up a fake one to make a point. Y'know? Errors, seek not. Stories, enjoy you should!|archivedate=20190918194118}}</ref> while [[Lucasfilm Story Group]]'s [[Matt Martin]] clarified that they are aware of it but it isn't an error as Yoda surely had more than a few lightsabers during his [[Time|lifetime]].<ref name="Martin's Tweet">{{Twitter|missingwords|status/1094999689349353473|[[Matt Martin]]|quote=Yup we were aware of that. It's not an error though, it was clear from RotS that he didn't leave Coruscant with that saber. A 900 year old Jedi has probably had more than a few sabers in his life.|archivedate=20200810122259}}</ref> The discrepancy was solved in ''[[Star Wars: The Book of Boba Fett]]'' episode "[[Chapter 6: From the Desert Comes a Stranger]]" with Yoda having [[Yoda's second lightsaber|another lightsaber]] in his possession.<ref name="BOBF 6">{{BOBF|Chapter 6: From the Desert Comes a Stranger}}</ref>
*The book mentions that after seeing [[Skywalker lightsaber|Anakin's lightsaber]] whilst in [[Ben Kenobi's home]], [[C-3PO]]'s protocol programming required him to request he shut down to protect his ex-owner's secrets. [[Pablo Hidalgo]] confirmed this information was supposed to have been cut from the book.<ref name="Pablo C-3PO">{{Twitter|pablohidalgo|status/1179652818837241856|[[Pablo Hidalgo]]|quote=I seem to recall it being a 2005 callout that should have been cut from the reprint but apparently wasn't.|archivefile=File:Pablo_C-3PO.png}}</ref>
*[[Bestine (city)|Bestine]] is stated to be the closest settlement to [[Ben Kenobi's home]]. This is contradicted by a map of [[Tatooine]] also seen in the book.

